	<h2>Campaign Setting</h2>
	<p>Each region of the golarion is unique; culture, economy, language, and societal structure vary greatly from
		one part of the world to another. Here are some important details to know about this campaign's setting:
	</p>
	<ul style="list-style-type:circle">
		<li style="list-style-type: none;"><b>Click to Show Campaign and Character Details:</b></li>
		<li style="list-style-type: none; display: none;"><b>Click to Hide Campaign and Character Details:</b></li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Citizenship:</b> Crusaders, pilgrims, and merchants journey from across the inner sea
			to the Worldwound in answer to the call of faith and coin, but few are from here, and most adventurers would come
			from amongst the native Kellid clansman of Sarkoris or the primary crusading nations. While many clansman hope to
			reclaim the wounded lands of their ancestral home, these people are displaced and without a nation. The people of
			Mendev make up the bulk of the crusaders armies and they offer citizenship to any who take up the cause,
			regardless of from whence they hail, often being the country of citizenship for sarkorans. Knights, people, and
			orders of Lastwall make up the next largest bulk, followed by the faithful traveling or escaping from Andoran,
			Taldor, Cheliax, and Galt, along with people from neighboring Ustalav and Numeria. There are also a fair few
			nomads from the realms of the horse and mammoth lords, though these tend to travel in their own companies rather
			than fully mingle with the rest.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Currency:</b> The confluence of so many nations has led to an exchange based primarily
			on weight, but nearly all coin are exchanged freely amongst the crusading towns along the worldwound. The
			predominant currency, is the Taldan standard copper, silver, gold, and platinum pennies, and nearly all standard
			coinage are referred to as the same, regardless of its mint origin. Aside from standard foreign currencies like
			Chelaxian silver ferries and Andoran gold sails, Taldan Ha'Pennies and Crowns, Numerian Silverdisks, and the
			Dwarven ingot-shaped talents can also be spotted and spent nearly as easily as anything else. However, while the
			cursed coinage of demonkind is used amongst them and their kin, they are not considered currency amongst the
			crusading lands and care should be taken to not accidentally engage in the arbitrage of the soul trade.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Languages:</b> Because of the vast international makeup of the crusading peoples, the
			Taldan Common tongue is the most used amongst the crusaders, though the local Hallit language is spoken by most of
			the Kellids native to Mendev and Sarkoris, and is more commonly spoken in private when away from the crowds of
			city squares and markets. Abyssal is also known to be the language of the enemy as is often learned by crusaders
			and cultist alike, while Celestial remains the language of the church and is often heard among the prayers of the
			faithful or those trying to appear particularly devout. Draconic, Dwarven, Elven, Halfling, Orc, and Undercommon
			are also the primary language of many crusaders of diverse ethnicities. A few secret languages (such as druidic),
			ancient languages, and magical communication - particularly <i>telepathy</i> and <i>speak with animals</i> - will
			both feature prominently, but cannot be achieved by all characters. Many powerful beings expect to be able to
			communicate through their favorite mediums, including magical means, and may feign incomprehension of languages
			they do not wish to converse in. Others may understand a language but not be able to speak it at all.
			Other useful languages for this campaign include: Aklo, Giant, Ignan, Infernal, Necril, Osiriani, Shadowtongue,
			Sylvan, and Terran, but these will not be encountered as frequently. Other languages may feature incidentally, but
			may not be the ideal choice when choosing a new one to learn.</li>
		<li style="display: none;"><b>Prejudice:</b> While all are welcome to take up arms in the crusade against the Abyss,
			but the tragedies of the Red Morning Massacre, as well as the subsequent inquisitions of the Third Crusade cast a
			long shadow in peoples memory. Tieflings, witches, the insane, and the deformed bear the brunt of this
			discrimination, out of fear that their heritage, pursuits, and health all bring them more naturally in line with
			the enemy. Other outsider-blooded adventurers, occult practitioners, summoners (or god-callers as the sarkorans
			call them), and followers of deities perceived to be less-righteous or less-zealous than the crusading faiths also
			bear a great deal more scrutiny, and these may all need to put up with enhanced security checks to prove they are
			not possessed or directed by demonic intent. Even the penitent few who have once looked upon the heart of the
			Abyss and turned away are often scorned, as the suspicion of heresy never truly fades. While this prejudice is
			rooted in fear, it is often justified as necessary vigilance for ensuring the safety and security of the
			crusaders, civilians, and refuges living along the Worldwound.</li>
	</ul>
</section>